WEBVTT

00:00:22.606 --> 00:00:24.942
Welcome back to the Clippie Awards,

00:00:25.025 --> 00:00:28.320
honoring the year's
most accomplished delivery boys.

00:00:28.403 --> 00:00:32.533
Our next category
is Best Newcomer on a Bicycle.

00:00:32.824 --> 00:00:34.701
Good luck, Dwight.

00:00:35.035 --> 00:00:39.456
And the nominees are
Johnny Jensen from Rent-A-Cat,

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:46.296
Nezzbin Zalgabarg, Ten-Minute Underwear,
and Dwight Conrad, Speedy Wig.

00:00:47.714 --> 00:00:50.008
And the Clippie goes to...

00:00:52.219 --> 00:00:54.346
Little Johnny Jensen!

00:00:59.142 --> 00:01:03.730
- Oh, don't feel bad, son.
- We couldn't be more proud of you.

00:01:03.814 --> 00:01:05.566
Unless, of course, you had won the award.

00:01:05.816 --> 00:01:08.485
Man, you're so lucky
to have your family here.

00:01:08.569 --> 00:01:10.070
The stupid professor
didn't even show up for me.

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:14.366
I'd just like to thank my awesome parents.

00:01:14.491 --> 00:01:17.160
We love you, Johnny!

00:01:18.203 --> 00:01:20.122
Now, to get all serious.

00:01:20.539 --> 00:01:23.500
It's time to pay tribute
to the brave delivery boys

00:01:23.584 --> 00:01:26.670
who gave their lives
this past year in the line of duty.

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:31.049
Gene Tinker, bus.

00:01:31.967 --> 00:01:34.303
Charles Kiley, train.

00:01:35.554 --> 00:01:38.056
Mike Pisarik, lion.

00:01:39.808 --> 00:01:42.227
They're on the truck to Heaven now.

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:49.443
Up next, the award for Best Delivery Boy,
Newspaper, Phone Book, or Miscellaneous.

00:01:49.526 --> 00:01:51.737
Miscellaneous, that's me.

00:01:52.279 --> 00:01:54.114
And the nominees are

00:01:54.198 --> 00:02:00.495
Gene Tinker, Charles Kiley,
Mike Pisarik, and Philip J. Fry.

00:02:01.246 --> 00:02:03.123
And the Clippie goes to...

00:02:05.417 --> 00:02:07.294
Philip J. Fry!

00:02:11.673 --> 00:02:13.342
Sign here.

00:02:15.427 --> 00:02:20.307
Wow! I'd like to thank the
Academy of Delivery Sciences,

00:02:20.390 --> 00:02:22.142
my lovely robot, Bender,

00:02:22.226 --> 00:02:26.230
and most of all, my dear nephew,
Professor Farnsworth.

00:02:28.398 --> 00:02:31.735
He always has time for me,
whether it's sending me on a delivery,

00:02:31.818 --> 00:02:35.531
or just pulling me aside
to tell me I'm doing a bad job.

00:02:35.614 --> 00:02:40.077
And why? Because he's family,
and family is always there for each other.

00:02:41.828 --> 00:02:45.415
I'm sorry, but I can't keep reading
this tissue of lies.

00:02:45.499 --> 00:02:49.670
The truth is, Professor Farnsworth
is a cold, heartless jerk!

00:02:53.507 --> 00:02:55.342
And the fish sticks were limp!

00:03:00.305 --> 00:03:04.226
Ooh. Hefty. You could really
bash in a skull with this thing.

00:03:04.309 --> 00:03:05.811
I know, right?

00:03:09.439 --> 00:03:13.652
What? Oh, your awards show.
I'm sorry I couldn't make it,

00:03:13.735 --> 00:03:15.988
but I had a very good reason.

00:03:16.113 --> 00:03:17.823
Perhaps you'd favor us with it?

00:03:17.990 --> 00:03:23.579
My pleasure. You see, I came down
with a searing case of "who-gives-a-crap?"

00:03:27.124 --> 00:03:29.001
I wish I had more living relatives.

00:03:29.084 --> 00:03:32.129
- What about the professor's parents?
- They're still alive?

00:03:32.212 --> 00:03:35.132
Sure, they exhibit all the telltale signs.

00:03:35.215 --> 00:03:39.428
Toenails growing, hearts pumping fluid,
the whole shmagoigle.

00:03:39.553 --> 00:03:43.182
So where do these fossils live?
In sedimentary rock?

00:03:46.727 --> 00:03:49.730
No. According to his next of kin file,

00:03:49.813 --> 00:03:53.483
they live in a virtual retirement home
on the Near-Death Star.

00:03:53.567 --> 00:03:57.946
I've been there.
Let's boldly go where we've gone before.

00:04:13.754 --> 00:04:16.173
Halt! Visitors are forbidden.

00:04:16.340 --> 00:04:20.219
Oh, really? Does that rule apply
to Clippie winners?

00:04:20.302 --> 00:04:22.888
I'm sorry, sir. Go right in.

00:04:24.348 --> 00:04:26.016
That's Philip J. Fry!

00:04:27.643 --> 00:04:33.065
Ned and Velma Farnsworth.
423, Shadybrook Lane. Let's hover-roll.

00:04:35.859 --> 00:04:39.363
Fry, you're so confident
and take-charge on this trip.

00:04:39.446 --> 00:04:40.614
It's kind of a turn-on.

00:04:40.697 --> 00:04:43.158
Not now, Leela.
I'm trying to meet old people.

00:04:57.506 --> 00:05:01.134
My God! It's full of geezers.

00:05:11.478 --> 00:05:12.938
Hey, why are they hooked up like that?

00:05:13.021 --> 00:05:15.691
Is it some kind of Craft-matic
adjustable death bed?

00:05:15.774 --> 00:05:17.776
Don't be ridiculous.

00:05:17.860 --> 00:05:20.529
Their bodies are
being used to generate electricity.

00:05:20.612 --> 00:05:23.574
The idea came from
an old movie called The Matrix.

00:05:23.657 --> 00:05:27.911
But wouldn't almost anything
make a better battery than a human body?

00:05:27.995 --> 00:05:30.455
Like a potato? Or a battery?

00:05:30.581 --> 00:05:32.708
Plus, no matter how much
energy they produced,

00:05:32.791 --> 00:05:35.210
it would take more energy
than that to keep them alive.

00:05:35.502 --> 00:05:40.716
I know, I know. It sounds absurd.
In fact, when The Matrix first came out,

00:05:40.799 --> 00:05:44.928
it seemed like the single crummiest,
laziest, most awful, dimwitted idea

00:05:45.053 --> 00:05:48.807
in the entire history of science fiction,
but it turned out to be true.

00:05:49.016 --> 00:05:51.435
- Who knew?
- Good work, writer of The Matrix.

00:05:53.020 --> 00:05:55.230
This is it, room 1119.

00:05:56.690 --> 00:06:00.527
Aw, it's my relatives. I'm gonna call them
Gram-gram and Shabba-doo.

00:06:00.694 --> 00:06:04.656
They look just like you, Fry.
Arms, legs, ugly.

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:07.784
Too bad we can't visit them
in their virtual world.

00:06:07.910 --> 00:06:09.745
We can. Just moisten your heads

00:06:09.828 --> 00:06:12.456
and put on these
real-to-virtual adapter caps.

00:06:15.626 --> 00:06:19.922
Mm. Electricity plus hats
with wires on them.

00:06:20.005 --> 00:06:21.465
Are you sure this is safe?

00:06:21.548 --> 00:06:24.218
It's not just safe, it's 40% safe.

00:06:36.563 --> 00:06:39.066
Bender is falling!

00:06:51.119 --> 00:06:52.579
Told you it was safe.

00:06:57.125 --> 00:07:00.921
Wow. So this is my relatives'
virtual old-folks home?

00:07:10.639 --> 00:07:12.307
There's only one word for it.

00:07:12.391 --> 00:07:15.060
Terrible. And also horrible.

00:07:15.644 --> 00:07:18.647
This is their room.
They're gonna be so excited to meet me.

00:07:22.109 --> 00:07:23.902
Hi. I'm your distant relative.

00:07:23.986 --> 00:07:28.657
Forget it, you flimflammer.
I can smell a scam a mile away.

00:07:31.285 --> 00:07:35.038
Reverse mortgages.
Get your reverse mortgages here.

00:07:35.122 --> 00:07:37.499
Come in, come in.

00:07:39.251 --> 00:07:41.461
So you're really my grandson, huh?

00:07:41.545 --> 00:07:45.465
Well, it's not quite that simple.
See, I got frozen, and your son,

00:07:45.549 --> 00:07:48.677
Professor Farnsworth, is my great-great
great-great-great-great...

00:07:48.760 --> 00:07:50.846
What?

00:07:50.929 --> 00:07:53.223
Yep, I'm your grandson all right.

00:07:53.307 --> 00:07:56.143
A grandson? My, you're a big boy.

00:07:56.476 --> 00:07:58.979
What are you, a junior in high school?

00:07:59.062 --> 00:08:00.397
Most recently, yes.

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:02.191
You look so thin.

00:08:02.274 --> 00:08:05.444
Can I offer you
some virtual ham casserole?

00:08:05.527 --> 00:08:06.862
Sounds delicious.

00:08:10.032 --> 00:08:14.077
Oh, nobody's eaten my food
like that in ages.

00:08:14.369 --> 00:08:17.206
I don't cook much
since we moved out of reality.

00:08:25.964 --> 00:08:28.091
And that's how I got this scar
above my eye.

00:08:28.175 --> 00:08:30.219
And this fork inside my lung.

00:08:30.302 --> 00:08:32.304
That's a doozy.

00:08:32.387 --> 00:08:35.766
Hey, look at this.
I can pull my thumb off.

00:08:35.933 --> 00:08:38.393
Stop, Shabba-doo, you're freaking me out.

00:08:38.602 --> 00:08:40.646
Don't let him get your nose.

00:08:43.649 --> 00:08:45.442
Oh, Lord.

00:08:46.318 --> 00:08:49.738
It's so nice to have more family.
All I had was the Professor,

00:08:49.821 --> 00:08:51.740
and he's kind of crotchety and moldy.

00:08:51.990 --> 00:08:54.076
You should have seen him
when he was little.

00:08:54.201 --> 00:08:57.246
My golly, he was crotchety and moldy.

00:08:57.329 --> 00:09:02.292
We miss him terribly, but it sure has
been nice spending time with you.

00:09:02.376 --> 00:09:04.795
It gets lonely and depressing around here,

00:09:04.878 --> 00:09:08.423
with no entertainment
except the rigged Bingo!

00:09:08.507 --> 00:09:09.883
Prove it, loser!

00:09:10.467 --> 00:09:13.220
Well, we should get going.
It was great to meet you, though,

00:09:13.303 --> 00:09:16.598
and hear about how bursitis
transcends physical existence.

00:09:17.558 --> 00:09:19.560
Keep up with your studies, huh?

00:09:20.102 --> 00:09:22.563
What? Oh, God, I'm still here?

00:09:24.231 --> 00:09:26.608
I'll miss you guys!

00:09:30.821 --> 00:09:35.033
I don't think Gram-gram likes that place.
And I know Shabba-don't.

00:09:35.158 --> 00:09:36.994
I sure wish they could come with us.

00:09:42.666 --> 00:09:44.626
- Elder abuse! Elder abuse!
- Elder abuse! Elder abuse!

00:09:44.710 --> 00:09:46.336
- Elder abuse! Elder abuse!
- Elder abuse! Elder abuse!

00:09:46.587 --> 00:09:47.671
Go it! Fastly!

00:09:49.923 --> 00:09:52.134
It won't start. The batteries are dead.

00:09:55.220 --> 00:10:00.475
I'm about to lose history's greatest life
all because of some useless old people.

00:10:00.559 --> 00:10:03.812
They're not useless.
They can cook ham casserole,

00:10:04.062 --> 00:10:08.192
and watch TV at incredible volume,
and they generate electricity.

00:10:08.275 --> 00:10:09.359
That's it!

00:10:25.167 --> 00:10:27.836
We can cut them off.
Turn left at Miriam Feinberg.

00:10:29.588 --> 00:10:30.672
I meant Feingold!

00:10:42.935 --> 00:10:45.771
It's so nice to meet
the Professor's parents.

00:10:46.021 --> 00:10:49.191
I'm Dr. Zoidberg. I'm very important.

00:10:49.274 --> 00:10:52.027
Hey, Zoidberg, you forgot
to empty this trash can.

00:10:52.110 --> 00:10:53.445
Don't hit me!

00:10:54.446 --> 00:10:57.407
I bet the Professor will be
thrilled to see you again.

00:10:57.533 --> 00:11:01.078
Professor. Some special
old people are here for you.

00:11:01.203 --> 00:11:03.872
Are they my zombies from
Hammacher Schlemmer?

00:11:03.956 --> 00:11:06.542
Mama? Daddy?

00:11:06.917 --> 00:11:08.961
- Son!
- Hey, kiddo!

00:11:09.628 --> 00:11:13.757
Leave me alone!
I never want to see you again!

00:11:15.175 --> 00:11:16.927
What crawled up his Geritol?

00:11:17.010 --> 00:11:19.054
Well, to be honest,

00:11:19.137 --> 00:11:22.015
a few troubling things
did happen in our past.

00:11:22.224 --> 00:11:25.310
Lady, all of human history
happened in your past!

00:11:27.813 --> 00:11:30.691
Let's not let those dark days
ruin our visit.

00:11:30.858 --> 00:11:34.319
Philip, what do you say we go
out today and live it up?

00:11:34.444 --> 00:11:38.657
"Live it up"? I'll be surprised
if you live it out!

00:11:38.740 --> 00:11:40.784
You're on fire!

00:12:20.657 --> 00:12:23.368
Whoa. Looks like you guys had fun.

00:12:23.577 --> 00:12:27.956
Did we ever! Gram-gram fell down at the
ice cream store and we got free ice cream!

00:12:28.248 --> 00:12:30.459
Now let's all go take a nap!

00:13:00.989 --> 00:13:03.617
Professor, Amy and I are concerned
that... Oh!

00:13:03.700 --> 00:13:05.327
Oh-ho-ho.

00:13:05.827 --> 00:13:07.663
Oh...

00:13:07.829 --> 00:13:09.748
Aw, what's wrong?

00:13:10.207 --> 00:13:14.419
I just want my parents to love me.
God, how I hate them!

00:13:14.503 --> 00:13:16.255
Of course they love you!

00:13:16.338 --> 00:13:19.842
Not like they love Fry!
They never played with me like that.

00:13:19.967 --> 00:13:23.720
They were always "too tired."
What awful parents!

00:13:23.929 --> 00:13:26.890
Come closer.
I'll tell you the whole story.

00:13:27.349 --> 00:13:28.976
Tell you what.
Let's get an opaque bubble layer going.

00:13:33.438 --> 00:13:34.523
Okay, go.

00:13:34.815 --> 00:13:37.693
It all started a century and a half ago.

00:13:39.069 --> 00:13:42.406
I had a perfectly normal adolescence
in New New York.

00:13:42.614 --> 00:13:45.200
But my parents were simple
hedge fund managers

00:13:45.284 --> 00:13:48.078
who couldn't appreciate my
interest in science.

00:13:48.328 --> 00:13:50.622
Mom, Dad, look what I made!

00:13:50.914 --> 00:13:54.459
That's wonderful, dear.

00:13:55.085 --> 00:13:57.004
Here you go, Squeakers.

00:13:58.755 --> 00:14:01.884
I was accepted to MIT at the age of 14,

00:14:01.967 --> 00:14:05.888
but my parents crushed my dreams
like a discarded frog head.

00:14:06.221 --> 00:14:08.891
Honey, I'm afraid we can't let you go.

00:14:08.974 --> 00:14:12.227
You're just not emotionally
mature enough for college yet.

00:14:12.311 --> 00:14:14.438
"Not mature enough..."

00:14:16.940 --> 00:14:21.695
Worried that city life was filling my head
with an unhealthy respect for education,

00:14:21.778 --> 00:14:25.699
my parents moved us to a peaceful farm.

00:14:28.202 --> 00:14:30.787
- I think that's sweet.
- Oh, shut up!

00:14:33.624 --> 00:14:36.126
You're all I have left, Squeakers.

00:14:39.463 --> 00:14:43.217
Oh, God, I hated that place.
My parents kept me there for years.

00:14:43.300 --> 00:14:48.180
So when I finally ran away,
I vowed never to speak to them again!

00:14:48.263 --> 00:14:51.558
That's a really sad
and long story, Professor.

00:14:51.642 --> 00:14:54.478
But you'll never get over this
unless you go to your parents now

00:14:54.603 --> 00:14:56.980
and tell them honestly how you feel.

00:14:57.981 --> 00:15:00.400
You're right. I will.

00:15:01.610 --> 00:15:06.406
I hate you, I hate you, I hate you!
You ruined my life! Goodbye!

00:15:14.540 --> 00:15:17.835
That is one crazy, uncircumcised old man.

00:15:23.257 --> 00:15:27.010
I almost headed off
the Professor at the East River,

00:15:27.094 --> 00:15:28.554
but the wind caught his skin flaps

00:15:28.637 --> 00:15:31.181
and sent him parasailing
over the Queensboro Bridge.

00:15:31.306 --> 00:15:34.059
Why would he go to Queens?
He doesn't need tires.

00:15:34.852 --> 00:15:36.937
Our old farm was in Queens.

00:15:37.020 --> 00:15:41.066
He must've gone back there to defile
it with his nudity! Come on, hurry!

00:15:45.988 --> 00:15:49.867
Why are you so eager to find the
Professor, Fry? Aren't you mad at him?

00:15:49.950 --> 00:15:52.327
Of course I'm mad.
That's been established.

00:15:52.411 --> 00:15:55.330
I just want to find him for
Gram-gram and Shabba-doo's sake.

00:15:55.789 --> 00:15:56.874
Who?

00:15:56.957 --> 00:15:59.001
Faster, faster! Okay, stop short!

00:16:03.338 --> 00:16:04.798
There's the old place.

00:16:06.383 --> 00:16:08.427
Hasn't changed a bit.

00:16:08.510 --> 00:16:10.762
Put on your glasses, sweetie.

00:16:17.936 --> 00:16:19.521
I hear something.

00:16:25.652 --> 00:16:26.778
It's the Professor.

00:16:27.863 --> 00:16:30.240
Yes, everyone look at the freak.

00:16:30.324 --> 00:16:32.075
His parents didn't love him,

00:16:32.159 --> 00:16:36.663
so they kept him here,
100 miles from the nearest microscope!

00:16:37.581 --> 00:16:39.833
Why? Why?

00:16:39.917 --> 00:16:44.546
Oh, he deserves the truth, Velma.
I think he's old enough to handle it.

00:16:45.255 --> 00:16:50.302
Cupcake, we never told you this,
but you had an older brother.

00:16:51.470 --> 00:16:53.180
Whaa...

00:16:54.473 --> 00:16:56.975
In many ways, he was like you.

00:16:57.059 --> 00:17:00.646
He loved science
and was desperate to go to college.

00:17:00.729 --> 00:17:03.023
He even looked a little like you.

00:17:05.150 --> 00:17:10.239
But the sad fact was...
How can I put this delicately?

00:17:10.447 --> 00:17:12.616
He was a crazy-ass nut job!

00:17:12.699 --> 00:17:15.160
Thank you, Ned. But so what?

00:17:15.244 --> 00:17:19.122
He was our crazy-ass nut job,
and we loved him.

00:17:21.083 --> 00:17:24.753
Every night, the poor boy
had terrors in his sleep.

00:17:24.962 --> 00:17:27.589
His pajamas would be soaked with sweat.

00:17:27.756 --> 00:17:29.967
Yeah, sweat. Right.

00:17:30.133 --> 00:17:32.970
We'd stay at his bedside all night.

00:17:33.053 --> 00:17:36.557
And whenever he'd start
moaning and sweating his pants,

00:17:36.640 --> 00:17:39.351
we'd read to him from his favorite book.

00:17:39.685 --> 00:17:45.732
Eighty-one, thallium.
Eighty-two, lead. Eighty-three, bismuth.

00:17:46.233 --> 00:17:49.403
The poor little psycho
never could've survived on his own.

00:17:49.486 --> 00:17:51.989
So we kept him on
the farm as long as we could.

00:17:52.322 --> 00:17:55.409
But one night, he ran away,

00:17:55.492 --> 00:17:58.078
taking nothing
but the clothes on his back.

00:17:58.161 --> 00:17:59.454
Woo-hoo-hoo!

00:18:07.087 --> 00:18:10.966
He was admitted
to a prestigious institution.

00:18:11.049 --> 00:18:12.384
A mental institution!

00:18:13.594 --> 00:18:18.765
Got himself a full wackademic scholarship.
And we never saw him again.

00:18:19.516 --> 00:18:22.269
We couldn't let you end up
like your older brother.

00:18:22.603 --> 00:18:24.855
That's why we kept you on the farm,

00:18:24.938 --> 00:18:28.358
and helped you get your
online doctorate in Rodeo Studies.

00:18:28.984 --> 00:18:30.485
Rodeo Studies?

00:18:30.652 --> 00:18:33.113
We did it out of love for you, Floyd.

00:18:33.447 --> 00:18:37.075
Floyd? Who the hell's Floyd? I'm Hubert!

00:18:37.284 --> 00:18:40.245
You're Hubert? The older brother?

00:18:41.705 --> 00:18:44.791
But we thought you were still
in an institution!

00:18:44.875 --> 00:18:48.587
Oh, no, no. I was out within 25 years.

00:18:48.670 --> 00:18:51.548
It felt like a minute
compared to grad school.

00:18:51.924 --> 00:18:55.636
Oh, Hubie, we're so happy
to see you again.

00:18:55.719 --> 00:19:00.265
Wait. Was all that true?
Did you really sit up with me every night?

00:19:00.390 --> 00:19:04.937
You bet, sport. That's why we were
always too tired to play with you.

00:19:05.020 --> 00:19:07.189
I'm so sorry we never got the chance.

00:19:09.274 --> 00:19:12.444
Oh, Mama! Daddy!

00:19:17.783 --> 00:19:21.578
This may not be the best time,
but a couple years ago,

00:19:21.662 --> 00:19:25.207
a homeless rodeo clown named Floyd
came to the door claiming he was...

00:19:25.290 --> 00:19:28.460
Bender! Why do you always have to
be the center of attention?

00:19:35.217 --> 00:19:38.971
You're sure you want to go back
to the Near-Death Star?

00:19:39.054 --> 00:19:43.308
My, yes.
Our muscles are sore, our bones ache.

00:19:43.392 --> 00:19:45.018
My damn skin even hurts.

00:19:45.102 --> 00:19:46.562
It's no dream house,

00:19:46.645 --> 00:19:50.566
but we get fed through
our spines and the rent is reasonable.

00:19:54.069 --> 00:19:56.738
Don't worry.
I'll come back and visit sometime.

00:19:56.822 --> 00:20:00.951
How about December, when they inject
the holidays into our brains?

00:20:01.159 --> 00:20:03.036
That sounds nice, Gram-gram.

00:20:03.954 --> 00:20:06.582
Well, son, I guess this is goodbye again.

00:20:06.665 --> 00:20:07.875
Not quite yet.

00:20:07.958 --> 00:20:11.837
I took the liberty of reprogramming
your retirement simulation.

00:20:13.213 --> 00:20:16.508
Why don't I come in for a moment
and show you around?

00:20:25.684 --> 00:20:29.271
Good virtual God! It's our old farm.

00:20:29.354 --> 00:20:31.190
It's beautiful.

00:20:31.273 --> 00:20:35.152
Appearances.
Just a simple matter of appearances.

00:20:35.235 --> 00:20:36.528
Speaking of which...

00:20:42.242 --> 00:20:45.370
My boy! My beautiful boy!

00:20:45.537 --> 00:20:49.124
One last chance
to play, if you're not too tired.

00:20:49.374 --> 00:20:52.336
Not too tired to chase you,
you cute little nut job!

00:20:52.419 --> 00:20:55.005
- Come on, Velma.
- Someone needs tickles!

00:20:55.088 --> 00:21:00.093
I'm gonna get you! Here I come!
I almost got you! Come here, you.